# Exercise: Spawning a Strand
#
# `strand.spawn` takes a quotation and runs it in a new strand.
#
# Stack effect: ( Quotation -- strand-id )
#
# The quotation runs concurrently. `strand.spawn` returns
# immediately with the strand's ID.
#
# Example:
#
#   [ "hello from strand" io.write-line ] strand.spawn
#   # main continues while the strand runs
#
# Closures and captured values
#
# Quotations in Seq are closures: when the `[` literal is
# evaluated, whatever's on top of the stack at that moment is
# captured *by copy* into the quotation's environment. The
# original value stays on the stack — capture doesn't pop it.
#
# That's why this works without any `dup`:
#
#   chan.make                          ( chan )
#   [ 42 swap chan.send drop ]         ( chan [quot] )    quot captured chan
#   strand.spawn                       ( chan strand-id ) spawn ate the quot only
#
# Both the spawned strand and main hold references to the same
# underlying channel: the spawned strand uses its captured copy
# to send 42; main uses the one still on the stack to receive.
#
# Your task: spawn a strand that sends 42 through a channel,
# then receive that value on the main strand.
#
# Stack hint: at the "your code here" marker below, the stack is
# `( chan )`. `chan.receive` consumes it and leaves ( 42 true );
# drop the flag and you're done.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-basics ( -- )
    chan.make
    [ 42 swap chan.send drop ] strand.spawn
    drop   # drop strand-id, leaving ( chan )
    # Your code here — receive the value and drop the flag.
    drop
    0
    # Do not edit below this line
    42 test.assert-eq
;
